%p
  Functional communication is a cornerstone of intervention for children with autism. Having an effective, efficient means of communicating with caregivers and peers is essential to meeting one’s needs, establishing and growing meaningful relationships, expressing feelings and ideas, and achieving personal goals. Functional communication is the foundation upon which practitioners and caregivers can help autistic individuals build skills to reach their potential, quality of life, and happiness.

%p
  Communication is one of the core skill deficits that defines autism spectrum disorder. Behavior analysts, caregivers and autistic individuals have many tools at their disposal to support individuals in developing communication skills, all of which come with strengths and weaknesses. The
  %a{ href: "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Picture_Exchange_Communication_System", target: "_blank" } Picture Exchange Communication System
  \ , developed by pioneering practitioners Lori Frost and Andy Bondy, is a manualized, evidence-based system that focuses on developing motivation to initiate communication and early manding skills using exchangeable pictures representing items and activities.

%p
  While PECS is manualized and meets the essential need to develop motivation to communicate, many families express frustration with making and maintaining physical materials, and not having all the right icons available in a given moment. Losing a picture, or stopping to flip through pages can derail motivation and lead to frustration.

%p
  With AAC apps and devices, many families express frustration with the learning curve of navigating pages, adding icons, and other aspects of using a robust app or device. For our earliest learners, or individuals experiencing barriers or the frustration associated with repeated failed attempts at developing functional communication, complex apps and devices may feel overwhelming. Barriers such as limited visual discrimination or scanning, or difficulty associating line drawings with real items, may present challenges. In addition, initiating or engaging in communicative exchanges may need to be established as motivating and meaningful.

%p
  All behavior is communication. Without a functional way to communicate needs and feelings, individuals may rely on challenging or harmful behavior to express their needs and feelings. Barriers such as those noted above may result in many failed attempts to develop a functional communication system, or may significantly delay the development of communication in our earliest learners.

%p
  I initially approached James with these concerns in mind, with lots of questions about the functionality of using an app to establish early communication skills. Why isn’t it easier to add a picture? Why can’t I make a new page? How could we make this easier and more accessible to families who desperately want to establish functional communication for their loved ones?

%p
  I am a long time proponent of PECS, and strong believer in the power of motivation. We want to create a foundation for building a robust AAC system, a springboard to more complex communication using any of the robust, individualized tools that make up the spectrum of AAC devices and supports. We want to make communication motivating and meaningful for our learners, and empower caregivers with a tool that won’t require a steep learning curve or substantial financial investment.

